---
import ComparisonLayout from '../../../layouts/ComparisonLayout.astro';
---

<ComparisonLayout
  slug="minimax-vs-lyria"
  a="minimax"
  b="lyria"
  title="MiniMax Music 3 vs Google Lyria 3: Self-Host or Vertex | Sonura"
  description="MiniMax Music 3.0 vs Google Lyria 3.5 and Lyria 3 Pro on length, languages, open weights, watermarking, and terms. Verified August 2026."
  keywords="minimax vs lyria, lyria vs minimax, minimax music 3, google lyria 3.5, lyria 3 pro vertex ai, open weights music model, self host music generation, synthid watermark"
  lede="if you are putting music generation inside something you are building, this is the real choice: an open checkpoint you host yourself, or a google cloud endpoint that was already approved. the audio is closer than the operating models are."
  verdict={[
    {
      label: 'Best to own outright',
      pick: 'minimax',
      body: 'open weights on hugging face, five-minute songs in one pass, and no api key, quota, or deprecation notice in the loop.',
    },
    {
      label: 'Best to deploy tomorrow',
      pick: 'lyria',
      body: 'vertex ai, eight languages, multi-vocal conditioning, and a contract your company has probably already signed.',
    },
  ]}
  headToHead={[
    {
      dimension: 'Length in one pass',
      a: 'five minutes, generated as a single coherent take with structure, vocal identity, and arrangement holding throughout.',
      b: 'three minutes on Lyria 3 Pro and thirty seconds on Lyria 3, which suits cues and components more than full records.',
      winner: 'a',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Languages',
      a: 'lyrics in, sung as written, but without explicit multi-language vocal conditioning as a stated capability.',
      b: 'eight languages with multi-vocal conditioning: english, german, spanish, french, hindi, japanese, korean, and portuguese.',
      winner: 'b',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Owning the pipeline',
      a: 'the checkpoint is on hugging face. it cannot be repriced, rate-limited, deprecated, or turned off underneath you.',
      b: 'a managed google endpoint. excellent uptime, and google decides what runs and for how long.',
      winner: 'a',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Getting to production',
      a: 'you provision gpu, you run inference, you keep it up. the 8B plus 0.6B stack is not a small deployment.',
      b: 'an api call. quotas, iam, billing, and regions are already solved, which is most of the actual work.',
      winner: 'b',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Terms and provenance',
      a: 'training data undisclosed, and a custom community licence you need to read against your specific use.',
      b: 'training data not disclosed in detail either, but covered by google cloud terms with SynthID watermarking applied.',
      winner: 'b',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Cost curve',
      a: 'fixed. gpu time, whether you generate ten tracks or ten thousand, which gets cheaper the more you use it.',
      b: 'per call. cheap to start, predictable, and it scales linearly with usage forever.',
      winner: 'tie',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Audio spec',
      a: '32 kHz 16-bit stereo wav, so plan an audio super-resolution pass before anything gets mastered.',
      b: 'delivered through vertex or flow at a spec suited to production use.',
      winner: 'b',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Control over the arrangement',
      a: '1 to 3,500 characters of lyrics, an auto-optimiser, instrumental mode, and named instruments and techniques.',
      b: 'prompt intros, verses, choruses, and bridges directly, with timed lyrics and the arrangement built around them.',
      winner: 'tie',
    },
  ]}
  narrative={[
    {
      heading: 'This is an infrastructure decision, not a taste one',
      body: `<p>almost nobody picks between these on how they sound. minimax music 3 landed on 17 august 2026 and lyria 3.5 on 29 july 2026, and both are strong. the decision is made somewhere else entirely: <strong>do you want to run a model, or call one?</strong></p><p>running one means gpu, deployment, monitoring, and an audio super-resolution stage to get above 32 kHz. calling one means an api key and a bill. the first buys permanence and unit economics at volume. the second buys a working feature this week. those are both correct answers to different questions about your team.</p>`,
    },
    {
      heading: 'The watermark cuts both ways',
      body: `<p>google applies SynthID watermarking to generated audio. if you are a platform and you need to demonstrate what was generated and what was not, that is a feature you would otherwise have to build. it survives normal processing and it is inaudible.</p><p>if your users are artists releasing under their own names, it is a permanent marker in the file, and you should tell them it is there rather than let them find out. minimax has no equivalent, which is either freedom or a missing provenance signal depending on which side of the product you sit on.</p>`,
    },
    {
      heading: 'Read the community licence properly',
      body: `<p>"open weights" reads like a settled question and it is not. minimax music 3 ships under a <strong>custom community licence</strong>, not a standard open one, and the training data is undisclosed. that combination is normal for this wave of releases and it still means somebody on your side has to read the actual document against your actual use.</p><p>lyria does not make you do that reading, because it inherits google cloud terms, which your legal team almost certainly signed years ago. that is not a better legal position, it is a legal position that costs you nothing to adopt, and for a lot of teams that is the deciding factor whether they say so or not.</p>`,
    },
  ]}
  chooseA={[
    'want the weights on disk so no vendor decision can end your pipeline',
    'generate at high volume and want a fixed cost rather than a per-call one',
    'need five minutes of song from a single pass',
    'have gpu capacity and someone to keep inference running',
    'can add an audio super-resolution stage above 32 kHz',
  ]}
  chooseB={[
    'want a working feature this week rather than a deployment project',
    'need vocals across eight languages with proper conditioning',
    'already run on google cloud and do not want a new vendor review',
    'want provenance watermarking built in rather than added later',
    'need short cues more often than full-length songs',
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      question: 'Should I self-host MiniMax Music 3 or use Google Lyria?',
      answer: 'self-host minimax if you generate at volume, need the weights permanently, and have gpu capacity plus someone to keep inference running. use lyria if you want a working feature quickly, need vocals across eight languages, or already run on google cloud so the vendor review is done. the audio quality gap is much smaller than the operational gap.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Is MiniMax Music 3 free to use?',
      answer: 'the weights are published on hugging face, so running it yourself costs hardware rather than subscription. it ships under a custom community licence rather than a standard open one, and the training data is not disclosed, so read the licence against your use before you release anything commercially.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How long can each one generate?',
      answer: 'minimax music 3 generates up to five minutes in a single pass. lyria 3 pro reaches three minutes and lyria 3 reaches thirty seconds. if your product needs full-length songs rather than cues, minimax has the clear advantage.',
    },
    {
      question: 'What audio quality does MiniMax Music 3 output?',
      answer: '32 kHz 16-bit stereo wav. that is a good mix and it is below the 44.1 or 48 kHz you want before mastering or distribution, so a realistic minimax pipeline includes an audio super-resolution pass after generation. budget for two models, not one.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Does Google watermark Lyria output?',
      answer: 'yes, google applies SynthID watermarking to its generated audio. that is useful if you need to demonstrate provenance downstream, and worth disclosing to your users if they are releasing music under their own names.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Do either give me stems?',
      answer: 'no. minimax returns a finished mix and lyria returns finished audio, and neither exports separate parts. sonura splits any track into stems on every paid plan if the output needs to be produced with rather than played.',
    },
  ]}
/>
